{"name":"Filtered Moon","hex":"#ecca9a","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"filtered","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["With a filter (e.g., a cigarette).","Having been filtered (e.g., coffee grounds)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of filter"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/filtered"},{"term":"moon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Moon (“the Earth's only permanent natural satellite”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any natural satellite of a planet.","The light received from the Moon; moonlight."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Moon","blocks":[{"text":"The Moon is the only natural satellite of Earth. It orbits Earth in an elliptical path, at a distance that varies over the year, averaging around 378,000 kilometers (235,000 mi). It completes an orbit in relation to Earth and the Sun (synodically) every 29.5 days. The Moon and Earth are bound by gravitational attraction, which is stronger on the sides facing each other. The resulting tidal forces are the main driver of Earth's tides, and have pulled the Moon to always face Earth with the same near side. This tidal locking effectively synchronizes the Moon's rotation period to its orbital period."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/330px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/500px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/1280px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/500px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFullMoon2010.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Big_Splash_Theia.gif/500px-Big_Splash_Theia.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Big_Splash_Theia.gif/500px-Big_Splash_Theia.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This not-to-scale animation shows (in an Earth and Sun stationary rotating frame of reference) the formation of the Moon through the collision of the hypothetical Mars-sized planet named Theia with Earth. After orbiting for tens of millions of years at the L4 Lagrange point as an Earth-trojan, Theia's orbit comes in \"loops\" finally too close to Earth.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABig_Splash_Theia.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Archean.png/500px-Archean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Archean.png/1280px-Archean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Archean.png/500px-Archean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Artist's depiction of the Moon in Earth's sky as it might have appeared around 4 billion years ago, after the Late Heavy Bombardment. At that time the Moon orbited the Earth at a third of its current distance, making it appear 2.8 times larger in apparent size than it does today.[60]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArchean.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Space.0076.fig.002.jpg/500px-Space.0076.fig.002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Space.0076.fig.002.jpg/500px-Space.0076.fig.002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Moon was volcanically active for billions of years after its formation. Lava flooding the lunar low lying terrane over time formed today's basalt maria, with their ages marked in this map (yellow to red: youngest; blue to purple: oldest).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpace.0076.fig.002.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Selected_Moons_of_Solar_System.jpg/500px-Selected_Moons_of_Solar_System.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Selected_Moons_of_Solar_System.jpg/1280px-Selected_Moons_of_Solar_System.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Selected_Moons_of_Solar_System.jpg/500px-Selected_Moons_of_Solar_System.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Size comparison of the main moons of the Solar System with Earth to scale. Nineteen moons are large enough to be round, several having subsurface oceans and one, Titan, having a considerable atmosphere.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASelected_Moons_of_Solar_System.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Youtubeastronautsonmoonot3.gif/250px-Youtubeastronautsonmoonot3.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/Youtubeastronautsonmoonot3.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Youtubeastronautsonmoonot3.gif/250px-Youtubeastronautsonmoonot3.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Astronaut John Young jumping on the Moon, illustrating that the gravitational pull of the Moon is approximately 1/6 of Earth's. The jumping height is limited by the combined weight of Young and his EVA space suit on the Moon of about 27 kg (60 lb) and by the suit's pressurization resisting the bending of the suit, as needed for jumping.[93][94]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYoutubeastronautsonmoonot3.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/20120927_surveyor7-levitating-dust.jpg/500px-20120927_surveyor7-levitating-dust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/20120927_surveyor7-levitating-dust.jpg/1280px-20120927_surveyor7-levitating-dust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/20120927_surveyor7-levitating-dust.jpg/500px-20120927_surveyor7-levitating-dust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Surveyor 7 observes levitating dust, a phenomenon named Lunar horizon glow can be seen","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A20120927_surveyor7-levitating-dust.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/11214_2022_871_Fig6_HTML.webp/500px-11214_2022_871_Fig6_HTML.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/11214_2022_871_Fig6_HTML.webp/500px-11214_2022_871_Fig6_HTML.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Observations and modeling of the Moon's sodium exosphere, tail and \"spot\".","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A11214_2022_871_Fig6_HTML.webp"}],"sounds":{"moon":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/w